---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.

timer_sleep() returns immediately if ticks <= 0. While interrups inactive briefly, it sets the current thread's wkup_ticks to start+ticks and inserts the thread into sleep_list in sorted order. Interrupts are enabled and sema_down() is called on its own sleep_sema which blocks the thread.

The timer interrupt handler increment ticks (per tick) and then looks at the end of sleep_list (for the lowest value wkup_ticks). While the specific threads wkup_ticks is <= ticks, it removes thread from the list and calls sema_up() on its sleep_sema, and is put on the ready list after unblocking the thread. It stops at the frist thread that requires waiting.

>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler?

The handler never scans the wholes list as sleep_list is always kept sorted. It only the checks the last element (the first to wake up thread) and wakes it if "wake up" time has arrive, and repeats until it stops at a thread whose wkup_ticks is higher than current ticks.

